{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And Hebron was built seven years</b> – Is it possible that Ham built Hebron for Canaan his youngest son before he built Zoan of Egypt for his eldest son? Rather, it was built with all its goodness as one of seven in Zoan, and he comes to declare the praise of the land of Israel — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 13:22). But what appears correct in my eyes by way of peshat is that Hebron is Kiryat Arba — that is, the great man among the Anakim, whose name was Arba, and he is the one who built it; therefore it is called by his name. As it is stated: \"And the name of Hebron formerly was Kiryat Arba, the great man among the Anakim, he\" (Joshua 14:15). And this man begot a son and called his name Anak, and after his name they were called Anakim. Therefore Scripture says that there in Hebron were Ahiman, Sheshai, and Talmai, the children of the Anak. \"And Hebron was built seven years before Zoan of Egypt\" — to say that Arba built it for his son Anak seven years before the building of Zoan of Egypt, which is ancient, in order to declare the extraordinary length of these men's lives: for just as they are extraordinary in their stature, so were their days longer than those of any man, because for their father Hebron was built before Zoan of Egypt, and several generations were folded within it from then until now. And it is possible that he further hints by what he said, \"Is it fat or lean?\" (Numbers 13:20), that Hebron is very ancient and yet still produces fat and large fruits as he reported regarding the cluster — and all the more so the new land, which is even fatter. And perhaps Arba is the father of Ahiman, Sheshai, and Talmai, and he was so called because the Anakim were four. And the verse that says: \"Kiryat Arba, the father of the Anak — that is Hebron\" (Joshua 15:13) — its meaning will be: the father of the Anakim. Therefore he said \"the Anak\" and \"the children of the Anak\" — the sons of the greatest among them, as he said: \"the great man among the Anakim, he\" (ibid. 14:15), and therefore the definite article ה appears in it. And they were called \"Anakim\" for the beauty of their stature, for they are necklaces (ענקים) for the throats of men, just as they were called \"sons of Elohim\" in the book of Genesis, as I explained there (Genesis 6:4). And this is the verse's meaning: Hebron was built seven years by this Anak before Zoan of Egypt. And because the builder of Hebron is well-known and famous — for so the city's name is Kiryat Arba — he said \"was built,\" or its meaning is that it was built for those who built it for themselves — Arba their father — and behold they lived a very great number of years from the day of its founding until now:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:05:00.010600073Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}